IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ea-core`
IMPORT `ea-definitions`
IMPORT `sgia-interpretation-time`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- "version in force from 5/12/2025" -- LAYER 1, ea3: THE FIVE SCHEDULES (lines 5513-5823 of EmA1968.txt).
--
-- Source text: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore Statutes Online
-- (inputs/EmA1968.txt).  It is not the official text.  Every `-- src:` line copies a line of that file.
-- The Third, Fourth and Fifth Schedules are tables that the converter flattened; where the place of a cell is a
-- reading and not a fact, NOTES.md says so (forks F13 to F19).  In the Third and Fifth Schedules the sign
-- `�` stands for the multiplication sign the converter lost (a `×` is where it survived).
--
-- These rules answer four seams of PLAN.md section 4 (SEAMS-ANSWERED.md lists them):
--   S6  the Third Schedule: `sch 3 the gross rate of pay for one day`, `sch 3 the basic rate of pay for one day` (MAYBE NUMBER)
--   S7  the Fourth Schedule: `sch 4 the hourly basic rate of pay` (MAYBE NUMBER)
--   S8  the Fifth Schedule: `sch 5 the number of days on which the female employee is entitled to absent herself` (MAYBE NUMBER)
--   and the First Schedule, which the keel's "workman" takes as `the First Schedule specifies the person` (MAYBE BOOLEAN).
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING means the
-- Act does not answer on these facts (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (ea3)").  Never read NOTHING as no.

§ `First Schedule -- Workmen`

-- The First Schedule is the list that limb (d) of "workman" in s 2(1) points to ("any person specified in the
-- First Schedule").  Items (1) to (7) name an occupation; item (8) names workmen paid piece rates in the
-- employer's premises, and so depends on being a workman by some other limb (fork F16).
-- src:5517 | WORKMEN
-- src:5518 | (1) Cleaners.
-- src:5519 | (2) Construction workers.
-- src:5520 | (3) Labourers.
-- src:5521 | (4) Machine operators and assemblers.
-- src:5522 | (5) Metal and machinery workers.
-- src:5523 | (6) Train, bus, lorry and van drivers.
-- src:5524 | (7) Train and bus inspectors.
-- src:5525 | (8) All workmen employed on piece rates in the employer’s premises.
@ref First Schedule items (1) to (8) (src:5517-5525)
DECLARE `sch 1 Kind of occupation` IS ONE OF
    `sch 1 item (1) cleaners`
    `sch 1 item (2) construction workers`
    `sch 1 item (3) labourers`
    `sch 1 item (4) machine operators and assemblers`
    `sch 1 item (5) metal and machinery workers`
    `sch 1 item (6) train, bus, lorry and van drivers`
    `sch 1 item (7) train and bus inspectors`
    `sch 1 an occupation that none of items (1) to (7) names`
    `sch 1 it is not known whether an item names the occupation`

@ref First Schedule items (1) to (8) (src:5517-5525)
GIVEN occupation IS A `sch 1 Kind of occupation`
      `the person is employed on piece rates in the employer's premises` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a workman by another limb of the definition in section 2(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 the person is specified in the First Schedule` MEANS
    CONSIDER occupation
    WHEN `sch 1 item (1) cleaners` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (2) construction workers` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (3) labourers` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (4) machine operators and assemblers` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (5) metal and machinery workers` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (6) train, bus, lorry and van drivers` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 item (7) train and bus inspectors` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 an occupation that none of items (1) to (7) names` THEN
        IF `the person is employed on piece rates in the employer's premises` AND `the person is a workman by another limb of the definition in section 2(1)`
        -- item (8): "All workmen employed on piece rates in the employer's premises"
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(d) any person specified in the First Schedule" points at the list and only at the list (fork F15)
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the occupation is not known to be inside or outside items (1) to (7), and the Schedule gives no test
    WHEN `sch 1 it is not known whether an item names the occupation` THEN NOTHING

§ `Second Schedule -- Registration fee`

-- The fee is stated for a claim by an employee and for a claim by an employer.  A claim by a subcontractor
-- for labour (s 115(3)) is in neither paragraph, so the Schedule states no fee for it (fork F17).
-- src:5534 | REGISTRATION FEE
-- src:5535 | The registration fee payable by any person making a claim with the Commissioner
-- src:5536 | for Labour is as follows:
-- src:5538 | (a) where the claim is made by an employee ... $3
-- src:5540 | (b) where the claim is made by an employer ... $20.
@ref Second Schedule paragraphs (a) and (b) (src:5534-5540)
GIVEN claimant IS A `Kind of claimant`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 2 the registration fee` MEANS
    CONSIDER claimant
    WHEN `Claimant who is an employee` THEN JUST 3
    WHEN `Claimant who is an employer` THEN JUST 20
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): paragraphs (a) and (b) name an employee and an employer; the Schedule states no fee for a subcontractor for labour
    WHEN `Claimant who is a subcontractor for labour` THEN NOTHING

